# Switching a player while reviewing mid-tree. CLAUDE.md's prose test plan
# ("Player Switch During Navigation") made executable.
#
# What could go wrong: activatePlayer() interrupts whoever is blocked in genmove
# and every enabled engine is supposed to stay at the same position as the
# record. A switch made mid-tree must therefore leave the cursor and the record
# exactly where they were, and navigation and play must both still work
# afterwards — a desynced engine shows up as a refused move or a truncated
# replay, not as an error.
#
# simple.sgf is 19x19, B[dd] W[pp] B[dp] W[pd], no result.

load_sgf tests/data/simple.sgf
wait_idle
switch_player black human
switch_player white human
wait_idle
expect main_line_moves 4

navigate_start
wait_idle
expect view_position 0

navigate_forward
navigate_forward
wait_idle
expect view_position 2
expect black_stones 1
expect white_stones 1

# --- The switch itself must be inert -----------------------------------------
switch_player white mock
wait_idle
expect white_player mock
expect view_position 2
expect main_line_moves 4
expect black_stones 1
expect white_stones 1
expect phase paused

# --- Navigation still works, in both directions ------------------------------
navigate_back
wait_idle
expect view_position 1

navigate_end
wait_idle
expect view_position 4
expect at_end true
expect black_stones 2
expect white_stones 2

# --- And so does play --------------------------------------------------------
# Space at the end of an unfinished branch asks the kibitz engine for a move.
# The engine has to be at the right position for this to succeed at all, which
# is the real assertion: a desynced engine rejects the move.
key space
wait_until main_line_moves >= 5
wait_idle
# Deliberately >=, not ==: White is an engine now, so once the kibitz move lands
# it is White to move and the match carries on by itself.
expect main_line_moves >= 5
expect phase playing
